---
import { getCollection, type CollectionEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import CaseDiagram from '../../components/CaseDiagram.astro';
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const entries = (await getCollection('work', ({ data }) => data.published)).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
  return entries.map((entry, index) => ({ params: { slug: entry.id }, props: { entry, prev: entries[index - 1], next: entries[index + 1] } }));
}
interface Props { entry: CollectionEntry<'work'>; prev?: CollectionEntry<'work'>; next?: CollectionEntry<'work'> }
const { entry, prev, next } = Astro.props;
const navEntry = (item?: CollectionEntry<'work'>) => item ? ({ id: item.id, title: item.data.title }) : undefined;
---
<CaseLayout title={entry.data.title} description={entry.data.summary} eyebrow={entry.data.eyebrow} status={entry.data.status} outcome={entry.data.outcome} prev={navEntry(prev)} next={navEntry(next)}>
  {entry.id === 'memory-efficient-distribution' && <>
    <section class="case-section glance"><p class="section-label">At a glance</p><div class="glance-grid"><div><small>Problem</small><p>Duplicate full-dataset materialization created memory pressure across export and serving.</p></div><div><small>My role</small><p>Investigation, option evaluation, POC validation, and architecture design.</p></div><div><small>Current status</small><p>Design validated through a POC; distribution and failure semantics remain in review.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">The problem</p><h2>Memory pressure was a property of the data path, not a single allocation.</h2><p>The existing flow scanned the distributed store, built a serialized artifact, and later loaded that artifact in the serving layer. Looking across the full lifecycle exposed the same core issue on both sides: the complete dataset was materialized in memory.</p><p>The immediate symptom was producer-side memory pressure and OOM risk. Treating only that symptom would have left the serving-side constraint intact.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">Understanding the existing system</p><CaseDiagram type="distribution-existing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Options considered</p><h2>The key decision was where lookups should happen.</h2><p>The evaluation considered serialization cost, memory behavior, artifact distribution, and latency implications of remote access. Remote membership lookups would simplify local state, but would move network dependency into a latency-sensitive path.</p><p>The preferred direction kept lookup data local while changing its representation and distribution model.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">Proposed architecture</p><CaseDiagram type="distribution-proposed" /></section>
    <section class="case-section validation"><div><p class="section-label">Validation</p><h2>POC evidence, not a production outcome.</h2><p>A production-scale validation tested whether a compact key representation and LMDB snapshot could materially improve storage efficiency.</p></div><dl><div><dt>~27 B → 8 B</dt><dd>Average identifier/key size to POC key representation</dd></div><div><dt>~43%</dt><dd>LMDB footprint reduction</dd></div><div><dt>~31%</dt><dd>Compressed snapshot reduction</dd></div></dl></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Trade-offs</p><h2>Local speed creates a distribution problem that must be designed explicitly.</h2><p>Read-only local snapshots keep network access outside the hot path and reduce runtime memory pressure. In return, the system must define snapshot publication, distribution, activation, rollback, and failure behavior. Those semantics remain part of the architecture review.</p><h3>My contribution</h3><p>I traced memory behavior across producer and consumer, evaluated alternatives, built and measured the LMDB POC, and proposed the snapshot-based design. The design is not presented here as production-deployed.</p></section>
  </>}
  {entry.id === 'removing-redundant-work' && <>
    <section class="case-section glance"><p class="section-label">At a glance</p><div class="glance-grid"><div><small>Problem</small><p>A high-volume path paid for sorted pagination without needing its additional guarantee.</p></div><div><small>My role</small><p>Investigation, equivalence validation, change, canary, and monitoring.</p></div><div><small>Result</small><p>Average production runtime fell from ~0.65s to ~0.15s after rollout.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">The symptom and investigation</p><h2>The expensive step was hidden behind a reasonable abstraction.</h2><p>The processing path was slower than expected. Following the work through its pagination layer showed that sorted pagination was doing additional work even though the underlying database access already provided the ordering needed by this use case.</p><p>The question was not whether ordering mattered in general, but whether this path needed the abstraction’s stronger guarantee.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">What changed</p><CaseDiagram type="processing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Decision</p><h2>Use the simpler pagination behavior where its contract is sufficient.</h2><p>After establishing that the stronger ordering behavior was redundant for this access pattern, I moved the path to regular pagination. The change removed work rather than shifting it elsewhere.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual compact"><CaseDiagram type="rollout" /></section>
    <section class="case-section result"><p class="section-label">Production result</p><div class="result-number"><span>~0.65s</span><i>→</i><strong>~0.15s</strong></div><p><b>~77% lower average production runtime</b>, observed after rollout and sustained through production monitoring.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Trade-off and lesson</p><h2>Evaluate abstractions against the guarantees a caller actually needs.</h2><p>Sorted pagination remains useful when its contract is required. Here, retaining it would have preserved unnecessary work. Output-equivalence checks, canary rollout, and production monitoring made the narrower choice safe to ship.</p><h3>My contribution</h3><p>I investigated the runtime, traced behavior into the pagination abstraction, validated output equivalence, implemented the change, and followed it through canary and production monitoring.</p></section>
  </>}
  {entry.id === 'shipping-new-format' && <>
    <section class="case-section glance"><p class="section-label">At a glance</p><div class="glance-grid"><div><small>Problem</small><p>A new format did not fit the assumptions of an existing serving pipeline.</p></div><div><small>My role</small><p>Serving-side design and implementation, guardrails, tests, and staged rollout.</p></div><div><small>Outcome</small><p>Launched successfully with no observed regression in the existing experience.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Why the format did not fit</p><h2>The pipeline’s existing model encoded assumptions the new format did not share.</h2><p>Extending the serving path required more than adding another value. The system needed to recognize the new inventory semantics early enough to apply format-specific matching without changing normal video behavior.</p><p>A dedicated model would provide cleaner long-term semantics, but would also expand the first release into a much larger cross-stack change.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Architectural choice</p><h2>Classify early, carry meaning internally, and isolate the behavioral branch.</h2><p>The serving-side design introduced an early classification step and an internal semantic signal. Existing pipeline stages could remain shared, while pause-specific matching stayed in a clearly bounded branch.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><CaseDiagram type="routing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Guardrails and backward compatibility</p><h2>The default path remained the existing path.</h2><p>Format-specific behavior was gated by explicit detection and isolated matching. Tests covered the new branch and protected existing behavior, while staged rollout created room to observe the system before broader exposure.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">Trade-off</p><CaseDiagram type="sequencing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Rollout and reflection</p><h2>Ship the smaller coherent change, while naming the debt it creates.</h2><p>The feature launched successfully through a staged rollout, with no observed regression in the existing experience. The incremental model enabled a faster first release, while deliberately leaving a future path toward cleaner cross-stack semantics.</p><h3>My contribution</h3><p>I led the serving-side implementation: classification and routing, isolated matching behavior, guardrails, tests, and staged rollout. The broader modeling trade-off was treated as a sequencing decision rather than a claim that the dedicated model lacked value.</p></section>
  </>}
</CaseLayout>
